%% Chapter 29: Action Semantics
\label{ch:action-semantics}

\section{Introduction to Action Semantics}
\label{sec:action-semantics-intro}

Action semantics, developed by Peter Mosses and David Watt in the 1980s and 1990s, is a semantic framework that combines the precision of denotational semantics with the concreteness of operational semantics. Rather than defining semantics through mathematical functions (as in denotational semantics) or state transitions (as in operational semantics), action semantics describes the meaning of programs in terms of \emph{actions}---abstract computational events that are composed using a rich algebra of combinators.

The motivation for action semantics was to provide a semantic framework that is both formally rigorous and accessible to practitioners. Traditional denotational semantics requires familiarity with domain theory and fixed-point mathematics. Operational semantics requires defining abstract machines and transition relations. Action semantics, by contrast, uses a vocabulary of computational concepts---actions, events, behaviors---that are closer to the programmer's intuition.

\begin{historical}
Action semantics was developed by Peter Mosses starting in the mid-1980s, with the first comprehensive presentation in his 1992 paper ``Action Semantics: A Tutorial.'' David Watt contributed significantly to the application of action semantics to programming language definitions. The framework was influenced by the work of Hoare on CSP (Communicating Sequential Processes), by the theory of categories, and by the desire to create a semantic notation that could be used in compiler generation tools. The action semantic workbench (ASW) was developed to support experimentation with action semantic definitions.
\end{historical}

\section{Actions, Declarants, and Yielders}
\label{sec:actions-declarants-yielders}

\subsection{Actions}
\label{sec:actions}

The fundamental concept in action semantics is the \emph{action}. An action represents a computational event---something that happens during the execution of a program. Actions can be sequential, concurrent, conditional, iterative, and so on.

\begin{definition}[Action]
\label{def:action}
An \textbf{action} is an abstract computational event that may:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Yield} a datum (produce a value)
  \item \textbf{Receive} a datum (consume a value)
  \item \textbf{Complete} (terminate normally)
  \item \textbf{Fail} (terminate abnormally)
  \item \textbf{Diverge} (not terminate)
\end{itemize}
Actions are composed using combinators that specify their sequencing, concurrency, and data flow.
\end{definition}

The basic actions include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\textbf{give } t$: yield the token $t$
  \item $\textbf{conceive}$: receive a token
  \item $\textbf{fail}$: fail immediately
  \item $\textbf{diverge}$: diverge (non-termination)
  \item $\textbf{complete}$: complete successfully
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Declarants}
\label{sec:declarants}

A \emph{declarant} represents a binding---the association of an identifier with a meaning. Declarants are used to model declarations, variable bindings, and scope.

\begin{definition}[Declarant]
\label{def:declarant}
A \textbf{declarant} is a mapping from identifiers to their meanings (types, values, or storage locations). Declarants are manipulated by actions that create, extend, and query bindings.
\end{definition}

Key operations on declarants include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\textbf{declare}(i, m)$: bind identifier $i$ to meaning $m$
  \item $\textbf{lookup}(i)$: retrieve the meaning bound to $i$
  \item $\textbf{scope}(a)$: restrict the scope of bindings created by action $a$
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Yielders}
\label{sec:yielders}

A \emph{yielder} is an action that yields a datum without performing any other computational effect. Yielders are the ``values'' of the action semantic world---they represent pure computations that produce results.

\begin{definition}[Yielder]
\label{def:yielder}
A \textbf{yielder} is an action that yields a datum $d$ and then completes. Yielders are the building blocks for data manipulation in action semantics.
\end{definition}

Examples of yielders:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\textbf{give } 42$: yield the integer 42
  \item $\textbf{give } x$: yield the value of variable $x$
  \item $\textbf{give } f(d_1, \ldots, d_n)$: yield the result of applying $f$ to the given data
\end{itemize}

\section{Facets of Action Semantics}
\label{sec:action-facets}

Action semantics is organized into three \emph{facets}, each addressing a different aspect of computation:

\subsection{Procedural Facet}
\label{sec:procedural-facet}

The \textbf{procedural facet} deals with the flow of control---the order in which actions are performed. It includes combinators for sequencing, choice, iteration, and concurrency.

Key combinators of the procedural facet:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $a_1 \textbf{ then } a_2$: sequential composition---perform $a_1$, then $a_2$
  \item $a_1 \textbf{ or } a_2$: nondeterministic choice---perform $a_1$ or $a_2$
  \item $a_1 \textbf{ and } a_2$: concurrent composition---perform $a_1$ and $a_2$ concurrently
  \item $\textbf{repeat } a$: iteration---repeat $a$ indefinitely
  \item $\textbf{while } b \textbf{ do } a$: conditional iteration
  \item $a_1 \textbf{ else } a_2$: committed choice---try $a_1$; if it fails, try $a_2$
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Declarative Facet}
\label{sec:declarative-facet}

The \textbf{declarative facet} deals with data and bindings---the manipulation of values and the association of identifiers with meanings. It includes operations for creating bindings, looking up values, and managing scope.

Key operations of the declarative facet:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\textbf{bind}(i, v)$: bind identifier $i$ to value $v$
  \item $\textbf{unbind}(i)$: remove the binding of identifier $i$
  \item $\textbf{local}(a)$: perform action $a$ with local bindings
  \item $\textbf{yield}(v)$: yield value $v$ as the result of a computation
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Reflective Facet}
\label{sec:reflective-facet}

The \textbf{reflective facet} deals with the observation and manipulation of the computational process itself. It allows actions to inspect and modify their own execution context.

Key operations of the reflective facet:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\textbf{current\_continuation}$: yield the current continuation
  \item $\textbf{throw}(k, v)$: transfer control to continuation $k$ with value $v$
  \item $\textbf{catch}(a, h)$: perform action $a$, catching exceptions with handler $h$
\end{itemize}

\section{Action Notation for Programming Languages}
\label{sec:action-notation}

\subsection{Defining Language Constructs}
\label{sec:defining-constructs}

Action notation provides a systematic way to define the semantics of programming language constructs. Each construct is defined by specifying the action it denotes.

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Assignment}
  \semS \sembrack{x := e} &= \textbf{yield } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then give } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then store } x
\end{semrules}

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Conditional}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{if } b \textbf{ then } s_1 \textbf{ else } s_2} &= \textbf{yield } \semB \sembrack{b} \textbf{ then } (\textbf{if true then } \semS \sembrack{s_1} \textbf{ else } \semS \sembrack{s_2})
\end{semrules}

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: While Loop}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{while } b \textbf{ do } s} &= \textbf{yield } \semB \sembrack{b} \textbf{ then } (\textbf{if true then } \semS \sembrack{s} \textbf{ then repeat} \textbf{ else complete})
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Block Structure and Scope}
\label{sec:action-blocks}

Block structure is handled by the declarative facet, which provides operations for creating and managing local bindings:

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Block}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{begin } D ; S \textbf{ end}} &= \textbf{local } (\semD \sembrack{D} \textbf{ then } \semS \sembrack{S})
\end{semrules}

The $\textbf{local}$ combinator creates a scope in which the bindings established by $D$ are visible during the execution of $S$ but are discarded when the block completes.

\subsection{Procedure Calls}
\label{sec:action-procedures}

Procedure calls involve binding parameters, executing the body, and returning results:

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Procedure Call}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{call } p(e)} &= \textbf{yield } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then bind parameter then } \semS \sembrack{\text{body of } p} \textbf{ then unbind parameter}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Exception Handling}
\label{sec:action-exceptions}

Exceptions are handled by the reflective facet, which provides operations for raising and catching exceptions:

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Exception Handling}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{try } s_1 \textbf{ catch } s_2} &= \textbf{catch } (\semS \sembrack{s_1}) \textbf{ with } \semS \sembrack{s_2}
\end{semrules}

\section{Comparison with Other Semantic Frameworks}
\label{sec:action-comparison}

\subsection{Action Semantics vs.\ Denotational Semantics}
\label{sec:action-vs-denot}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{p{3.5cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Action Semantics} & \textbf{Denotational Semantics} \\
\hline
Foundation & Actions and combinators & Mathematical functions \\
Readability & High (intuitive vocabulary) & Low (requires domain theory) \\
Compositionality & Via action combinators & Via function composition \\
Fixed points & Implicit (via repeat) & Explicit (via $\mu$ or $\textbf{fix}$) \\
Implementation & Direct (action interpreter) & Indirect (via domain equations) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of action and denotational semantics}
\label{tab:action-vs-denot}
\end{table}

\subsection{Action Semantics vs.\ Operational Semantics}
\label{sec:action-vs-opsem}

Action semantics shares with operational semantics the idea of describing computation as a sequence of steps. However, action semantics provides a higher level of abstraction: actions are composed using combinators rather than defined through transition rules.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Abstraction level}: Action semantics operates at a higher level than SOS, using combinators rather than individual transitions.
  \item \textbf{Compositionality}: Action semantics is compositional (the meaning of a compound is determined by the meanings of its parts), while SOS is often non-compositional.
  \item \textbf{Formality}: Both are formal, but action semantics uses a more structured algebraic framework.
  \item \textbf{Implementation}: Action semantics can be directly interpreted (an action interpreter executes actions), while SOS requires an abstract machine.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Action Semantics vs.\ Attribute Grammars}
\label{sec:action-vs-ag}

Action semantics and attribute grammars both provide frameworks for syntax-directed semantics, but they differ in their approach to control flow:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Control flow}: Action semantics explicitly models control flow through action combinators. Attribute grammars model computation through attribute dependencies.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic behavior}: Action semantics can model dynamic behavior (loops, recursion, concurrency). Attribute grammars are primarily suited to static analysis.
  \item \textbf{Evaluation}: Action semantics is executed by an action interpreter. Attribute grammars are evaluated by traversing the parse tree.
\end{itemize}

\section{Practical Applications}
\label{sec:action-applications}

\subsection{Language Definition}
\label{sec:action-langdef}

Action semantics has been used to define the semantics of several programming languages, including:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A definitional interpreter for Standard ML
  \item Semantic definitions for subsets of Java and C\#
  \item Domain-specific languages for scientific computing
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compiler Generation}
\label{sec:action-compiler-gen}

The action semantic workbench (ASW) provides tools for generating compilers from action semantic definitions. The generated compiler performs:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Parsing (using a parser generator)
  \item Semantic analysis (by evaluating the action semantic specification)
  \item Code generation (by translating actions to target code)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Language Prototyping}
\label{sec:action-prototyping}

Action semantics is well suited to language prototyping because:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Semantic definitions are executable (they can be run as interpreters)
  \item The notation is relatively accessible to programmers
  \item Changes to the language design can be quickly reflected in the semantics
\end{itemize}

\section{Formal Foundations}
\label{sec:action-foundations}

\subsection{Action Algebras}
\label{sec:action-algebras}

The formal foundation of action semantics is an \emph{action algebra}---an algebraic structure that captures the essential properties of actions and their combinators.

\begin{definition}[Action Algebra]
\label{def:action-algebra}
An \textbf{action algebra} is a structure $(A, +, \cdot, ;, ^*, 0, 1)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $(A, +, 0)$ is a join-semilattice (nondeterministic choice)
  \item $(A, \cdot, 1)$ is a monoid (sequential composition)
  \item $;$ is a residual operation (implication)
  \item $^*$ is an iteration operator
\end{itemize}
satisfying certain axioms that capture the algebraic properties of actions.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Denotational Semantics of Actions}
\label{sec:action-denot}

Action semantics can be given a denotational semantics by mapping actions to mathematical objects. The standard denotation maps actions to \emph{behavior functions}:

\[
\sembrack{\cdot} : \textbf{Action} \to (\textbf{Trans} \to \mathcal{P}(\textbf{Trans}))
\]

where $\textbf{Trans}$ is the domain of transitions (input/output events) and $\mathcal{P}$ is the powerdomain. This denotational semantics provides a formal basis for reasoning about action semantic definitions.

\section{Detailed Examples}
\label{sec:action-examples}

\subsection{Complete Language Definition}
\label{sec:action-complete-lang}

To illustrate the full power of action semantics, consider a complete definition of a simple imperative language with variables, assignment, conditionals, loops, and blocks.

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Complete Language}
  \semP \sembrack{D ; S} &= \semD \sembrack{D} \textbf{ then } \semS \sembrack{S} \\
  \semD \sembrack{\textbf{var } x : T} &= \textbf{allocate variable of type } T \textbf{ then bind } x \\
  \semS \sembrack{x := e} &= \textbf{yield } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then store } x \\
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{if } b \textbf{ then } s_1 \textbf{ else } s_2} &= \textbf{yield } \semB \sembrack{b} \textbf{ then } (\textbf{if true then } \semS \sembrack{s_1} \textbf{ else } \semS \sembrack{s_2}) \\
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{while } b \textbf{ do } s} &= \textbf{iterate } (\textbf{yield } \semB \sembrack{b} \textbf{ then } (\textbf{if true then } \semS \sembrack{s} \textbf{ else fail})) \\
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{begin } D ; S \textbf{ end}} &= \textbf{local } (\semD \sembrack{D} \textbf{ then } \semS \sembrack{S}) \\
  \semE \sembrack{x} &= \textbf{yield } x \\
  \semE \sembrack{e_1 + e_2} &= \textbf{yield } \semE \sembrack{e_1} \textbf{ then yield } \semE \sembrack{e_2} \textbf{ then add}
\end{semrules}

This definition demonstrates how action semantics handles:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Declarations}: The declarative facet manages variable allocation and binding.
  \item \textbf{Assignment}: The procedural facet sequences evaluation and storage.
  \item \textbf{Control flow}: The procedural facet handles conditionals and iteration.
  \item \textbf{Scope}: The declarative facet's \textbf{local} combinator manages block structure.
  \item \textbf{Expressions}: The declarative facet handles value computation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Action Semantics for Object-Oriented Features}
\label{sec:action-oo}

Action semantics can also model object-oriented features such as classes, inheritance, and dynamic dispatch:

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Method Invocation}
  \semE \sembrack{e.m(e')} &= \textbf{yield } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then yield } \semE \sembrack{e'} \\
                           & \quad \textbf{then lookup method } m \\
                           & \quad \textbf{then bind self and parameter} \\
                           & \quad \textbf{then execute method body} \\
                           & \quad \textbf{then unbind and yield result}
\end{semrules}

Dynamic dispatch is handled by the \textbf{lookup method} action, which searches the class hierarchy at runtime to find the most specific method implementation.

\subsection{Action Semantics for Exceptions and Continuations}
\label{sec:action-exceptions-cont}

The reflective facet provides elegant semantics for exceptions and first-class continuations:

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Exceptions and Continuations}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{try } s_1 \textbf{ catch } s_2} &= \textbf{catch } (\semS \sembrack{s_1}) \textbf{ with } \semS \sembrack{s_2} \\
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{throw } e} &= \textbf{yield } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then throw} \\
  \semE \sembrack{\textbf{call/cc } e} &= \textbf{yield current continuation then yield } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then apply}
\end{semrules}

The \textbf{catch} combinator installs an exception handler, and \textbf{throw} transfers control to the most recently installed handler. The \textbf{call/cc} action reifies the current continuation as a first-class value.

\subsection{Action Semantics for Concurrency}
\label{sec:action-concurrency}

The procedural facet's \textbf{and} combinator provides concurrent composition:

\begin{semrules}{Action Semantics: Concurrent Composition}
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{cobegin } s_1 \textbf{ coend}} &= \semS \sembrack{s_1} \textbf{ and } \semS \sembrack{s_2} \\
  \semS \sembrack{\textbf{sync } e} &= \textbf{yield } \semE \sembrack{e} \textbf{ then synchronize}
\end{semrules}

The \textbf{and} combinator executes its operands concurrently, with synchronization handled by the \textbf{synchronize} action. This provides a simple model of concurrent execution that can be extended with more sophisticated synchronization primitives.

\section{Formal Semantics of Action Combinators}
\label{sec:action-formal}

\subsection{Denotational Semantics of Actions}
\label{sec:action-denot-formal}

The formal semantics of action combinators can be given by mapping actions to behavior functions:

\begin{definition}[Behavior Function]
\label{def:behavior-function}
A \textbf{behavior function} maps a transition (input event) to a set of transitions (output events):
\[
\sembrack{\cdot} : \textbf{Action} \to (\textbf{Trans} \to \mathcal{P}(\textbf{Trans}))
\]
where $\textbf{Trans}$ is the domain of transitions and $\mathcal{P}$ is the powerdomain.
\end{definition}

The semantics of the key combinators is:
\begin{align*}
  \sembrack{a_1 \textbf{ then } a_2}(\tau) &= \bigsqcup \{ \sembrack{a_2}(\tau') \mid \tau' \in \sembrack{a_1}(\tau) \} \\
  \sembrack{a_1 \textbf{ or } a_2}(\tau) &= \sembrack{a_1}(\tau) \cup \sembrack{a_2}(\tau) \\
  \sembrack{a_1 \textbf{ and } a_2}(\tau) &= \sembrack{a_1}(\tau) \parallel \sembrack{a_2}(\tau) \\
  \sembrack{\textbf{repeat } a}(\tau) &= \lfp(\lambda f.\, \lambda \tau.\, \sembrack{a}(\tau) \bind f)
\end{align*}

where $\parallel$ is the concurrent merge operator and $\bind$ is the monadic bind for the powerdomain.

\subsection{Operational Semantics of Actions}
\label{sec:action-operational}

Action semantics can also be given an operational semantics in terms of an abstract machine:

\begin{definition}[Action Machine]
\label{def:action-machine}
The \textbf{action machine} is an abstract machine with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A \textbf{control} component (the current action)
  \item A \textbf{transition} component (the current input/output event)
  \item A \textbf{binding} component (the current environment)
  \item A \textbf{storage} component (the current store)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The transition rules of the action machine define how actions are executed:
\begin{semrules}{Action Machine Transitions}
  (a_1 \textbf{ then } a_2, \tau, \beta, \sigma) &\to (a_1, \tau, \beta, \sigma) & \text{if } a_1 \text{ is not complete} \\
  (\textbf{complete}, \tau, \beta, \sigma) &\to (a_2, \tau, \beta, \sigma) & \text{continue with } a_2 \\
  (\textbf{fail}, \tau, \beta, \sigma) &\to \textbf{error} & \text{action failed}
\end{semrules}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:action-summary}

Action semantics provides a unique approach to programming language semantics that bridges the gap between formal rigor and practical accessibility. Its key contributions include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item A vocabulary of computational concepts (actions, declarants, yielders) that is closer to programmer intuition than domain theory.
  \item A compositional framework for defining language semantics using action combinators.
  \item Three facets (procedural, declarative, reflective) that organize semantic concerns.
  \item Executable semantic definitions that can serve as interpreters.
  \item Tools for compiler generation from semantic specifications.
\end{itemize}

While action semantics has not achieved the widespread adoption of denotational or operational semantics, it remains an important contribution to the theory of programming language semantics, particularly for language definition and compiler generation.

\begin{historical}
The development of action semantics reflects a broader trend in programming language research toward practical, tool-supported semantic frameworks. Key milestones include: Mosses's initial formulation (1980s); the tutorial presentation (1992); the action semantic workbench (1990s); applications to ML, Java, and C\# (1990s--2000s); and ongoing work on integrating action semantics with modern language development tools. The influence of action semantics can be seen in modern semantic frameworks such as the K framework and the Ott tool.
\end{historical}
